But that wouldn't be a nethack playing AI. I mean, when I think of a
program that plays nethack, I think something that follows the
strategies of a human player. I don't do the above, and I kind of
doubt most people do.
 
> Someone else suggested an automatic sokoban solver.  Anybody who was
> serious about making an automatic nethack player could start there and
> generate a great deal of credibility that the rest can be done.  

I did this once. Mostly for practice writing little state-space
exploration solvers, and then as a challenge when I realized how HARD
Sokoban is. The state space gets very large unless you pull some
tricks to remove redundant information. Even then, one of the versions
of the second level in Nethack's sokoban has the property that you
have to do a certain move at the beginning or the level is unsolvable,
but you can't tell the game is unsolvable until after you finish a
most of the rocks. My final implementation needed a few hundred MBs of
memory to solve that one, even after giving up on lowest-cost and
adding a few heuristics to determine when a game was lost. Notice that
this means solving the sokoban level in the abstract, not actually
moving the character and dealing with creatures that pop up, and so
on.


I would think that a nethack-player would not be solely based on
exploring outcomes of actions (e.g. exploring a state space like in
chess), but rather in having a list of strategies/goals and a very
limited state-space explorer for the inmediate decisions. So, if it
sees a floating eye, it knows it wants to eat one (from the goal of
acquiring ESP), but it would also know not to attack it in melee
(because of the state-explorer showing it'd become paralized).

Granted it would not be an infallible player, but it should be
possible to write somehting that plays as well as a newbie/mid level
player.

